The two artifacts in this directory are compiled into the binary with go:embed.
Both are fetched from the Hugging Face repository Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2 at
revision 751bff37182d3f1213fa05d7196b954e230abad9.

    model.onnx      onnx/model.onnx      90387606 bytes
                    sha256 759c3cd2b7fe7e93933ad23c4c9181b7396442a2ed746ec7c1d46192c469c46e
    tokenizer.json  tokenizer.json         711661 bytes
                    sha256 da0e79933b9ed51798a3ae27893d3c5fa4a201126cef75586296df9b4d2c62a0

That repository is an ONNX export of sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2,
Apache 2.0, six layers and 384 dimensions, published at a 256-token inference
cap. The float32 export is the one used: the int8 export is a quarter of the
size, and what it costs in the cosine geometry the class floors are calibrated
against has not been measured.

To verify:

    curl -sL https://huggingface.co/Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2/resolve/main/onnx/model.onnx | shasum -a 256
    curl -sL https://huggingface.co/Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2/resolve/main/tokenizer.json | shasum -a 256

exemplars.bin is built rather than fetched: it holds one vector per exemplar in
corpus.go, computed by this model, and is regenerated with `go test -update`. It
carries a fingerprint of the exemplar text, so a binary whose corpus has moved
since falls back to embedding the exemplars at run time rather than scoring
against stale vectors.
